FIX-FORWARD, one anchor. REPRODUCED THE DEFECT FIRST: `git grep -n 'ceiling.*EXHAUSTED' src/lib/rateLimit.js` exits 1 with no output, exactly as the verifier reported. Cause confirmed at rateLimit.js:719-725: the log is a template literal whose opening ends '...deployment ceiling (${ceiling.limit} scans per window, shared by ' at :720 and whose remainder 'is EXHAUSTED: anonymous scans' begins at :721, so no line-based pattern can carry both words in that order.

THE ONE CHANGE: docs/OPERATOR_LEDGER.md:287 now reads `git grep -n 'warnedCeilingExhausted' src/lib/rateLimit.js`. I RAN IT; verbatim output:
  src/lib/rateLimit.js:41:let warnedCeilingExhausted = false;
  src/lib/rateLimit.js:47:  warnedCeilingExhausted = false;
  src/lib/rateLimit.js:717:  if (deployment && !deployment.allowed && !warnedCeilingExhausted) {
  src/lib/rateLimit.js:718:    warnedCeilingExhausted = true;
exit 0, four hits. I chose the latch over 'is EXHAUSTED' because the sentence's claim is 'the log says so ONCE PER PROCESS', and the latch is what makes that true: :717 is the gate, :718 the set, :41 the declaration, :47 the test reset — the reader lands one line above the logger.warn at :719. Grepping the message text would have landed on the string but proved nothing about the once-ness. Prose rewrapped from '— `grep`. The' to '— the latch is / `grep`. The answer to' purely to keep the longer symbol under the file's ~80-col wrap; no claim changed. Diff is 3 insertions, 2 deletions in one file.

ALL OTHER ANCHORS RUN, NOT ASSERTED. I extracted every `git grep` line from the SHIPPED file text (not from memory) and executed each; all 8 return output, none exits 1. My three: rateLimitKnobsReadBy -> rateLimit.test.mjs:713,:730 (2 hits); warnedCeilingExhausted -> 4 hits above; 'every OPEN gated task' -> operatorLedgerGates.test.mjs:86 (1 hit). Caveat (a) from my last note is now DISCHARGED — that file was untracked at submit time, it is tracked as of commit 1f528f3, so the grep resolves. The five pre-existing anchors also resolve (INSERT INTO schema_migrations 1, PGRST204 1, RESTORE-TRUST-R0.1.20 6, export async function createScanJob 1, scanStoreUnavailable 3).

SHOULD A GUARD CHECK THE LEDGER'S OWN GREPS? YES, and I deliberately did NOT add it. It is the right shape — parse every `git grep -n '<pat>' <path>` out of the doc, run it, fail naming any that returns nothing — and it is the only thing that would have caught this class, which this ledger has now failed four docs tasks for. I did not add it because (i) my file scope this round is docs/OPERATOR_LEDGER.md and nothing else, and it needs a new .test.mjs, and (ii) it is a mechanism worth designing rather than smuggling into a one-line fix: it must decide whether it shells out to git (slow, and red on a legitimately untracked WIP file, which is precisely how caveat (a) arose) or re-implements matching, and whether an anchor pointing at a deleted file is red or a skip that must be visible. Recommend it as its own task; I have written the failing-first evidence into this note so whoever takes it does not have to rediscover the defect.

NOT DONE, deliberately: .env.example, rateLimit.test.mjs and operatorLedgerGates.test.mjs are untouched (all passed; git diff shows one file). I did not touch the header amendment note at :14-15 or the section-E prose at :447, because those are exactly the lines R6.2.5 is filed against and my anchor fix does not reach them — the self-immunisation the verifier found is untouched and still real.

TESTS: rateLimit.test.mjs + operatorLedgerGates.test.mjs in isolation 55 tests / 55 pass / 0 fail. Full npm run test:scoring in the shared tree: 2142 tests, 2116 pass, 0 FAIL, 26 skip — the four failures I reported last round were other agents' in-flight edits and are gone. Note test:scoring is 'node --test', not vitest; running vitest against these paths falsely fails because it also globs .claude/worktrees copies. No commit, no verify, nothing staged, no file outside scope touched. No secret was read or printed; .env.example was not opened this round.
